     1	# arXiv 2601.07809 — implicit equation under hidden reparametrization
     2	
     3	| profile field | value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B** — no-tool compression, not a complexity-hardness claim |
     6	| Native domain | algebra |
     7	| Object regime | rational exact |
     8	| Computational core | polynomial identity |
     9	| Certificate | homogeneous ternary polynomial |
    10	| Native objects | rational parametrized plane curve, ternary polynomial over Q, projective coordinate map, polynomial map of the parameter line |
    11	| Intended intuition | change of variables: use the unchanged low Taylor frame instead of implicitizing the high-degree parametrization |
    12	| Domain essentiality / reduction | native / none |
    13	
    14	## What the problem is
    15	
    16	[Orevkov, *On curves of degree 10 with 12 triple points*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07809), Proposition 1(a), gives an explicit rational parametrization of an irreducible degree-10 plane curve and its homogeneous Cartesian equation. An instance presents a high-degree polynomial parametrization obtained from that curve by a common covering of the parameter line, three independent coordinate scalings, and a shear. The solver must return the normalized homogeneous degree-10 polynomial that vanishes on it. The answer is a sparse integer polynomial, not indices or a graph surrogate.
    17	
    18	The generator samples the covering and coordinate map first and pulls the paper's equation through the inverse map. It therefore knows the answer by composition of identities. `verify` checks the answer's exact bounded syntax, reconstructs the coordinate map from the Taylor frame, expands the paper's polynomial through it, recovers the common covering from the full target parametrization, recomputes all target coefficients, and compares exactly. It never reads `inst["answer"]`, uses floats, or calls a solver.
    19	
    20	## Why Track B
    21	
